  "question": {
    "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> decompose via first order kinetics with half-lives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>54</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>min</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>min</mi></math> respectively. Starting from an equimolar non-reactive mixture of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, the time taken for the concentration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> to become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> times that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is ______&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>min</mi></math>. (Round off to the Nearest Integer)."
  },
